\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Director: Peter Greenaway\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Prospero \f1\endash  John Gielgud \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Miranda \endash  Isabelle Pasco\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Caliban \f1\endash  Michael Clark\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Antonio \f1\endash  Tom Bell\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Gonzalo \f1\endash  Erland Josephson \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Alonso \endash  Michel Blanc \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Sebastian \endash  Kenneth Cranham \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ferdinand \endash  Mark Rylance \f0\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}\f1 Prospero\rquote s Books, 1991 film\f0\u8239? \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
    Prospero\rquote s Books is very much an adaptation of Shakespeare\rquote s play. Initially, Gielgud speaks every line of the play alongside the actor playing the other characters. Thus Greenaway\rquote s premiss is dependent on the idea that Prospero is a portrait of Shakespeare himself, but Shakespeare in the film is transformed into a Renaissance mage. (Much of the imagery of the film is drawn from Old Master paintings, most notably St. Jerome in his Study by Antonella da Messina, c.1474.) \par
\par
    In the film, Prospero is Shakespeare himself, conceiving, designing, rehearsing, directing and performing the story's action as it unfolds and in the end, sitting down to write the completed work. \par
\par
    The \ldblquote spirits\rdblquote  of the island are represented by a huge number of extras, all of whom are naked throughout. This allows Greenaway to refer time and again in the film to Old Master paintings. \par
\par
    The film is formally innovative, combining\u8239?mime, dance, opera, and\u8239?animation. The films editing makes extensive use of digital image manipulation often overlaying multiple moving and still pictures with animations.   \par
\par
    Ariel is played by three young boys. The opening scene sees the youngest of these urinate on a paper boat, mimicking the tempest of the play\rquote s title. \par
\par
