{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Prospero \f1\endash  Roger Allam \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Miranda \endash  Jessie Buckley \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ariel \endash  Colin Morgan \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Caliban \endash  James Garnon\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ferdinand \f1\endash  Joshua James \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\tab\par
    Allam\rquote s \ldblquote Prospero seems a kindly, sardonic bookish presence, keenly aware that his project might founder [at] any moment. His rich voice, comic timing and touching anxiety over the blooming relationship between his daughter Miranda and the shipwrecked Ferdinand make him poignantly humane, as does his evident regret as he relinquishes his power\'85\rdblquote  (FT review, 5/5/2013, Sarah Hemming) \par
\par
    Jessie Buckley\rquote s Miranda is feisty, keen to reclaim her own personal history from her father in I.ii and outraged at his refusal to share details of their earlier life in Milan with her. \par
\par
    \ldblquote What we don\rquote t get is an engagement with the deeper, darker waters of the play, with Prospero\rquote s troubling treatment of Caliban and Ariel\'85There\rquote s the smugness of the colonial who assumes that his control is justified by his assumed superiority, but nothing too disturbing\'85\rdblquote  (Hemming)   \par
\par
***************************************************************************\par
\par
2010 film\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Director:\u8239?Julie Taymor\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Prospera \f1\endash  Helen Mirren \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Miranda \endash  Felicity Jones \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ariel \endash  Ben Whishaw \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Caliban\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9\endash  Djimon Hounsou\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Antonio \f1\endash  Chris Cooper\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Gonzalo \f1\endash  Tom Conti \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Alonso \endash  David Strathairn \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Sebastian \endash  Alan Cumming \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ferdinand \endash  Reeve Carney \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Trinculo \endash  Russell Brand  \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Stephano \endash  Alfred Molina \f0\tab\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
    Helen Mirren is \ldblquote Prospera\rdblquote , the gender switch \ldblquote doing the play no violence\rdblquote  according to The New Yorker\rquote s David Denby. \par
\par
    The Guardian\rquote s film critic Philip French was of the opinion that while Mirren\rquote s casting \ldblquote necessitates a little fiddling with the text, which is more than made up for by Mirren's commanding presence, the sensitive delivery of her lines and the way she imbues with warmth and humour a character usually played as bitter and aloof\'85\rdblquote   \par
\par
    But, according to French \ldblquote\'85the villains do not come over with any force: the low-life figures (Alfred Molina and Russell Brand) are as unfunny as ever, the romantic young couple are insipid, and the magnificent Beninese actor Djimon Hounsou as Caliban is robbed of his natural dignity. The special effects are intrusive and anything but magical and the text is rather curiously edited\'85\rdblquote  (The Guardian, Philip French, 6/3/2011)  \par
\par
    Michael Wood admired Mirren\rquote s performance, too: \par
\par
\ldblquote\'85she handles one difficult moment with perfect, thoughtful, understated calm. This is another crux in the play, where Prospero, effectively, forgives or pretends to forgive his foes because he doesn\rquote t want to seem less human than an inhuman spirit. Ariel, touched by the plight he has been instructed to create for the villains, says that if Prospero saw these bewildered victims his \lquote affections would become tender\rquote , adding: \lquote Mine would, sir, were I human.\rquote  When Mirren delivers the next line \f1\endash  \lquote And mine shall\rquote  \endash  she manages to drain it of all competition or reluctance; she\rquote s worked out why Ariel might be right. In fact, her affections don\rquote t become tender \endash  tenderness is not Mirren\rquote s line \endash  but she does recognise both Ariel\rquote s claim to his own feeling and his knowledge of how humans ought to feel. She is saying she will do what she can, even if she isn\rquote t generally fond of humans\'85\rdblquote  (London Review of Books, Michael Wood, 31/3/2011) \par
\par
    Wood also loved the film\rquote s ending:  \par
\par
\ldblquote\'85The [final] credit sequence refers back not to the scene just before it, where Prospero, or in this case Prospera, throws her staff off a rocky cliff, but to her earlier promise, intensely delivered over a tight close-up of her regretful eyes: \lquote I\rquote ll drown my book.\rquote  The last part of\f0\u8239?The Tempest\u8239?often feels strange in reading and performance because Prospero\rquote s forgiveness of his enemies seems so fake, a matter of formal pardon that allows undying hatred to rage on as it likes, but here the emphasis is all on the abandonment of magic. No spells will ever come from that sinking book again, and the renunciation is real in a way the forgiveness is not. It\rquote s not clear why the magician has to give up this power, but she unmistakably believes she has to \f1\endash  it\rquote s the price of a return to the world beyond the island, and her daughter\rquote s resumption of civilised life\'85.\rdblquote  (Wood) \par
\par
    Wood agreed with French about the comedic parts: \par
\par
\ldblquote\'85as almost always in modern versions of Shakespeare, whether on film or on stage, the comedians aren\rquote t funny, they are just doing what they (and their directors) imagine other people thought was funny. It\rquote s true that Stephano and Trinculo, in The Tempest, are not supposed to be only funny \endash  they are murderous too \endash  but this makes their pretend funniness all the more weakening. Perhaps they\rquote re going to commit a pretend murder too\'85\rdblquote  (Wood)  \par
\f0\lang2057\par
\lang9 ***************************************************************************\par
\f1\par
