{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ariel \endash  Rachel Hannah Clarke \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Caliban \endash  Ciaran O\rquote Brien\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Antonio \f1\endash  Patrick Osborne\f0\u8239? \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Alonso \f1\endash  Katy Stephens \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Sebastian \endash  Lucy Phelps \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Ferdinand \endash  Reeve Carney \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Trinculo \endash  Ralph Davis  \par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Stephano \endash  George Fouracres \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\tab\par
\par
    Prospero\rquote s enchanted island is presented as a run-down holiday resort, where the guests are boorish and the staff mutinous. \par
\par
    \ldblquote\'85The [production] concept is clear: western tourism is a kind of cultural and economic imperialism, local people smiling through gritted teeth as they cater to the whims of visitors who exoticise and exploit their country\'85\rdblquote  (Sam Marlowe, The Times, 1/8/2022) \par
\par
    The uniforms worn by Ariel and Caliban suggest their subordinate status \f1\endash  they seem to be employees of the hotel or workers on a cruise line. Ferdi Roberts\rquote s Prospero wears speedos and little else throughout. \par
\par
    Caliban is no \ldblquote savage and deformed slave\rdblquote  but is rather a brutally overworked staff member. \par
\par
    Miriam Gillinson claims that \ldblquote what\rquote s missing is the magic \endash  and the power and grace that the suggestion of sorcery lends to Shakespeare\rquote s language. Prospero\rquote s book of spells is bound together with a Tesco bag\'85\rdblquote  (Miriam Gillinson, The Guardian, 1/8/2022)  \par
\par
    We see the play open on the Italian aristocracy and their courtiers enjoying a boozy party while trapped in a Perspex box before the shipwreck \ldblquote sees them vomited up onto the stage from shipping containers filled with tiny plastic ducks. Besuited and bantering, [the Italians are] corporate wide-boys whose office jolly has gone awry\'85\rdblquote  (Marlowe) \par
\par
    Stephano and Trinculo are depicted as drunken Brits abroad. Ariel \ldblquote enchants\rdblquote  them with the offer of fast food while inviting them to join in a rendition of the football anthem Three Lions. \par
\par
    Marlowe describes the production as \ldblquote a DayGlo romp\'85leav[ing] little space for emotional engagement and none for wonderment\'85\rdblquote   \par
\par
